exercise
========

Implementation of a transaction processor: it reads a CSV file of
transactions, applies them to client accounts and writes the final balances
as CSV.


Usage
-----

    cargo run -- transactions.csv > accounts.csv

The input file is the only argument. Balances are written to standard output,
sorted by client id. On failure the program prints the error message on
standard error and exits with the errno.h code of the error: for example
ENOENT for a missing input file, EISDIR for a directory, EPIPE when the
output is closed, and EINVAL for a wrong argument count. An error without an
OS code exits with EIO.


Layout
------

    src/amount.rs   Amount: fixed-point decimal with four places.
    src/engine.rs   Engine: accounts, disputable deposits, transaction rules.
    src/lib.rs      CSV input and output around the engine.
    src/main.rs     Command line entry point; owns the engine state.
    tests/cli.rs    End-to-end tests running the binary on tests/data/.


Design choices
--------------

1. Amounts are integers

   An amount is a u64 holding a number of ten-thousandths: 1.5 is stored as
   15000. Arithmetic is exact, so there are no floating point rounding
   errors. Every addition and subtraction is checked: an overflow or a
   negative result is an error instead of a wrapped value. A u64 holds up to
   about 1.8e15 units, far beyond any realistic balance.

   Parsing is strict. It accepts "2", "2.", "2.5" and ".5" and rejects
   negative values, signs, exponents, separators, more than four decimals and
   values that do not fit. Output drops trailing zeros, so 1.5000 is written
   "1.5" and 2.0000 is written "2".

2. Every transaction is all or nothing

   Each transaction is checked completely before any state changes. A
   rejected transaction leaves the engine exactly as it was and returns a
   TxError giving the reason (locked account, insufficient funds, unknown
   transaction, ...). The binary ignores these errors, as the specification
   asks, but the reasons make the engine easy to test and to debug.

3. The total is derived, never stored

   An account stores only available, held and locked. The total is computed
   as available + held, so the three values can never disagree. A deposit is
   accepted only if the new total fits in a u64; no other operation increases
   the total, so computing it can never overflow.

4. Only deposits are kept in memory, and only while disputable

   The engine keeps two hash maps: client id -> account, and transaction id
   -> deposit (client, amount, disputed flag). Withdrawals are not stored:
   they cannot be disputed. A deposit is removed once its dispute is resolved
   or charged back, so a deposit can be disputed at most once and memory only
   holds what can still change state. Lookups are O(1).

5. Rules for dispute, resolve and chargeback

   - The referenced transaction must be a tracked deposit of the same client.
     A reference to a deposit of another client is treated as unknown.
   - A dispute is rejected if the deposited funds were already withdrawn,
     since holding them would make the available funds negative.
   - A dispute of a deposit already under dispute is rejected.
   - A resolve or a chargeback of a deposit that is not under dispute is
     rejected.

6. A chargeback freezes the account

   After a chargeback the account is locked and rejects every further
   transaction: deposits, withdrawals, disputes, resolves and chargebacks.

   A frozen account cannot be unfrozen: the specification defines no
   transaction that unlocks an account, so the lock is permanent. As a
   consequence, funds of other deposits still under dispute when the account
   is frozen stay held forever, since their resolve or chargeback is
   rejected too.

7. Failures create no accounts

   An account is created by its first successful deposit. A withdrawal from
   an unknown client fails without creating an empty account, so the output
   only lists clients that really had funds.

8. Input is streamed and bad rows are skipped

   The CSV is read one row at a time into a single reused buffer, so memory
   grows with the number of accounts and disputable deposits, not with the
   size of the file. Columns are matched by header name, surrounding
   whitespace is trimmed and the amount may be left out on dispute, resolve
   and chargeback rows.

   A row that cannot be understood is skipped and processing continues: an
   unknown type, an invalid client or transaction id, a deposit or withdrawal
   with a missing or invalid amount. A deposit reusing the id of a deposit
   still tracked is also rejected. Only an I/O error stops processing.

9. Readable code, one instantiation per function

   The engine is free of any I/O, which keeps the rules in one place and lets
   them be tested directly. main creates the Engine itself and passes it
   explicitly to process (read the CSV) and then to write_accounts (print
   the balances), so the state being worked on is always visible. The CSV functions take &mut dyn Read and
   &mut dyn Write instead of generic parameters: there is a single compiled
   copy of each, and coverage tools measure it as a whole.


Tests
-----

    cargo test
    cargo llvm-cov --summary-only

The unit tests check each rule of the engine, including that rejected
transactions leave the state unchanged, the amount parser and formatter, and
the CSV handling (whitespace, missing columns, invalid rows, read and write
errors). The tests in tests/cli.rs run the binary end to end. Coverage is
100% of regions, functions and lines.
